40625, -0.0207061767578125, -0.00579071044921875, -0.03765869140625, -0.036834716796875, -0.0169219970703125, -0.036712646484375, 0.05035400390625, 0.0290374755859375, 0.0203094482421875, 0.01910400390625, 0.0199432373046875, -0.0016355514526367188, -0.0013532638549804688, -0.048583984375, 0.0030345916748046875, 0.0026035308837890625, 0.006587982177734375, 0.028076171875, -0.0213623046875, 0.007648468017578125, -0.01392364501953125, -0.0157318115234375, -0.004810333251953125, 0.014495849609375, 0.001983642578125, 0.0198822021484375, -0.00257110595703125, 0.0029239654541015625, -0.0131072998046875, 0.0179901123046875, -0.005615234375, 0.021392822265625, -0.004795074462890625, 0.0017786026000976562, 0.01171875, -0.0243988037109375, -0.00585174560546875, -0.004924774169921875, -0.01335906982421875, -0.0209808349609375, -0.01140594482421875, 0.01300811767578125, -0.037689208984375, 0.016448974609375, 0.0050811767578125, 0.011199951171875, -0.00374603271484375, 0.004070281982421875, -0.01555633544921875, 0.0036563873291015625], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "How level-0 players behave and how they are perceived by higher-level players are central questions in the literature on level-k models of boundedly rational strategic reasoning. This study aims to investigate how level-0 actions and beliefs are formed and whether they align, as well as how these patterns differ across games, thereby testing the assumptions underlying standard level-k models. To address these questions, we apply the intra-team communication method developed by Burchardi and Penczynski (2014), which elicits players\u2019 reasoning processes to identify level-0 actions and beliefs. \n\nWe examine two games. First, in the canonical beauty contest game, where players typically choose numbers in an attempt to guess a fraction of the average choice, Burchardi and Penczynski (2014) found that elicited level-0 beliefs closely match observed level-0 actions, supporting the standard level-k assumption that level-0 players act non-strategically and are perceived as such by others. We replicate this finding, confirming the alignment between beliefs and actions for level-0 players in this context.\n\nSecond, we study a variant of the 11-20 game, originally introduced by Goeree et al. (2018), in which players select an integer between 11 and 20, with payoffs structured to reward choosing a number exactly one less than another player's choice. In our variant, we modify the payoff structure to further incentivize strategic reasoning, though the core mechanics remain similar. Goeree et al. (2018) observed that behavior in the original game often deviates from standard level-k predictions. In our study, we find that, unlike in the beauty contest game, elicited level-0 beliefs and observed level-0 actions in the 11-20 game variant diverge from each other and from the standard level-0 assumption. This divergence suggests that the standard level-0 specification may not fully capture the reasoning processes in this setting and points to the need for alternative or additional explanations beyond those previously proposed in the literature.\n\nThe observed divergence between beliefs and actions in the 11-20 game variant is significant for level-k modeling because it challenges the assumption that level-0 behavior is both non-strategic and commonly understood by higher-level players. This finding highlights the importance of context in shaping level-0 reasoning and suggests that level-k models may require refinement to account for such discrepancies.\n\nIn summary, our study demonstrates that the alignment between level-0 beliefs and actions is context-dependent, supporting standard assumptions in some games but not others, and underscores the need for more nuanced modeling of boundedly rational behavior in strategic settings."}}